<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amadeus V, Count of Savoy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1249–<a href="/years/1323">1323</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Amadeus the Great · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amadeus V, called the Great, was Count of Savoy from 1285 until his death in 1323. He extended Savoyard rule, gaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bresse">Bresse</a> through his marriage to Sibylle of Baugé, and in 1294 settled his nephew Philip with the family's lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, the origin of the Savoy-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> line. He was active in imperial and French politics and strengthened the county's position on both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amadeus-v-count-of-savoy.jpg" alt="Amadeus V, Count of Savoy" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknow person · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_di_Amedeo_V_di_Savoia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amadeus was the son of Thomas II of Savoy and Beatrice Fieschi, and through his mother a grandnephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a>. After his marriage to Sybille of Bâgé in 1272 he entered the service of his cousin, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a>, as a household knight. He served in the First Welsh War of 1277, and in the Second Welsh War of 1282 he commanded Edward's forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> that relieved the siege of Rhuddlan Castle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His childless uncle, Count Philip I of Savoy, died in 1285, and Amadeus's elder brother Thomas III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> had died by accident in 1282. Amadeus was awarded the County of Savoy. To lessen family rivalry, his younger brother Louis received the new Barony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a>. Through his wife, Countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugey">Bugey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bresse">Bresse</a>, he added those Burgundian districts to his lands. On 1 October 1285 he was declared protector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> after negotiations with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. In 1287 he captured the castle of Ile, near Geneva, after a siege of fourteen weeks, and his victories over Amadeus II of Geneva and Humbert I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dauphin-of-france">Dauphin of Viennois</a>, led to the Treaty of Annemasse on 18 November 1287, by which both became his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>.</p><p>In 1295 he acquired the fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a>, and he brought Georges de Aquila, a student of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> to decorate the castle. During the Gascon War of 1294 to 1303 he acted as an agent of Edward I in the talks that led to the Treaty of Montreuil in 1299 and the Treaty of Paris in 1303. In 1301 he settled a dispute over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a> with the diocese of Sion. His reign also saw friction between Savoy and the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alarmed by the recovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> by the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Amadeus sought a powerful ally in the German king Henry VII, who was married to the sister of Amadeus's second wife. He accompanied Henry on his Italian campaign of 1310 to 1313, which ended with Henry's coronation as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> on 29 June 1312. As a reward he became Imperial Count and imperial vicar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and received the lordships of Asti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivrea">Ivrea</a>. Henry also raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta">Aosta</a> and Chablais to duchies, though they stayed part of Savoy.</p><p>In 1315 Amadeus helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> against the Turks. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> on 16 October 1323. By his first wife he had eight children and by his second wife, Marie of Brabant, whom he married in 1297, four more. His son Edward succeeded him, then Edward's brother Aymon, and a daughter, Anna, married the Byzantine Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-iii-palaiologos">Andronikos III Palaiologos</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amadeus V of Savoy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1294 Count <b>Amadeus V</b> of the County of Savoy settled his nephew Philip with the family's lands in Piedmont as a dependent appanage.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-piedmont">Lordship of Piedmont</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Château du Bourget, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.65,5.87&amp;year=1297" aria-label="Château du Bourget on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4689 554 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Amadeus V, Count of Savoy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5139 824h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5139 824h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.65,5.87&amp;year=1297">What was here in 1297? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
